        "content": "URANUS, CRONUS AND HERMES, ADEPTS OF THE PERFECT SCIENCE\n\nAnd so it appears that he [Cronus] was not born from Heaven (which is impossible), but from that man who was called Uranus ; and that this is so, Trismegistus bears witness, when, in stating that there have been very few in whom the perfect science has been found,\n\n1 Was, however, this the spelling found in Cicero, for Firmianus takes it from the text of Tully ? It is a pity we have no critical apparatus of the text of Lactantius, for the MSS. of Cicero present us with the following extraordinary list of variants : Then, Ten, Their, Thoyt, Theyt, Theyn, Thetum, Them, Thernum, Theutatem, Theut, Thoyth, Thoth. See n. 5 to the text of Cicero, cited above. C/. R. 117, n. 2.\n\n2 Which he probably took from P. S. A., xxxvii. 4: \"Whose home is in a place called after him.\"\n\n3 Chambers (p. 41, n. 1), in referring it to C. H., v. (vi.) 10, is mistaken.\n\nhe mentioned in their number Uranus, Cronus and\n\nHermes, his own kinsfolk.1\n\ni\n\nHi. Ibid., ii. 8, 48; Brandt, p. 138; Fritzsche, i. 89.\n\nDIVINE PROVIDENCE\n\nFor the World was made by Divine Providence, not to mention Thrice-greatest, who preaches this.2"
